    "badge": "Best Ready-to-Drink",
    "category": "Bottled ultra-filtered milk shake",
    "description": "Core Power starts from ultra-filtered, pasteurized milk, which matters in pregnancy because unpasteurized dairy is off the table. Per retailer listings at the time of writing, a 14 fl oz bottle carries about 26 g of milk protein in about 170 calories, with around 5 g of milk sugar, no added sugar and roughly 650-670 mg of calcium. It is lactose-free. Listings show it sweetened with a mix of monk fruit, stevia, sucralose and acesulfame potassium, so check the bottle.",
    "pros": [
      "No mixing; drink it cold straight from the fridge on queasy mornings",
      "About 26 g of protein plus roughly half a day's calcium, per listings",
      "Pasteurized and lactose-free"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Contains sucralose and acesulfame potassium per listings",
      "Costs more per gram of protein than any tub here",
      "A 14 oz bottle is a lot of liquid to finish when nausea is bad"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The convenience pick for the fridge at work or home, especially when cold drinks are the only thing that goes down.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "About 26 g per bottle",
      "Calories": "About 170",
      "Third-party testing": "None we could confirm",
      "Best for": "Grab-and-go, nausea days"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "truvani-organic-plant-based-protein",
    "name": "Truvani Organic Plant Based Protein Powder",
    "brand": "Truvani",
    "badge": "Best Organic Plant",
    "category": "USDA Organic pea and pumpkin seed blend",
    "description": "Truvani's protein is built for label readers. The vanilla version lists organic pea protein, organic vanilla, organic pumpkin seed protein, organic monk fruit extract and organic chia seed protein, with about 20 g of protein in roughly 120-140 calories per serving, per retailer listings at the time of writing. It is USDA Organic, and the brand states the formula excludes soy, dairy, gluten and stevia.",
    "pros": [
      "Five-ingredient vanilla formula with no herbs, greens or stimulants",
      "USDA Organic and free of dairy and soy",
      "No stevia, which many people find bitter during pregnancy"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Plant proteins carry a higher average heavy-metal risk; ask Truvani for current batch results",
      "No sport certification we could confirm",
      "Earthy texture; most buyers blend it rather than shake it"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The plant pick for a dairy-free pregnancy, provided you check the brand's latest contaminant testing first.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "About 20 g (listing)",
      "Calories": "About 120-140",
      "Third-party testing": "USDA Organic; no sport certification confirmed",
      "Best for": "Dairy-free, organic"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Protein Powder for Pregnancy",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-protein-powder-for-pregnancy/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it safe to drink protein shakes while pregnant?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most women, a plain, third-party tested protein powder is considered fine in pregnancy, but there is no blanket approval and you should check with your OB or midwife. The main risks come from contaminants such as lead, from herbal or stimulant add-ins, and from high doses of vitamin A, not from the protein itself. Pick a short label with independent testing and keep it to one serving a day."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein do I need when pregnant?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Institute of Medicine sets the pregnancy RDA at 1.1 g per kilogram of body weight, about 71 g a day for a reference woman, compared with about 46 g before pregnancy. The increase applies mainly to the second and third trimesters. Some newer research suggests needs could be higher late in pregnancy, around 1.5 g per kilogram, so aiming a little above the RDA is reasonable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best protein powder for pregnancy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Our top pick is Ritual Essential Protein Daily Shake Pregnancy and Postpartum, because it was designed for pregnancy, gives 20 g of pea protein and 250 mg of choline per serving per its label, and contains no herbs or stimulants. If you prefer dairy, Thorne Whey Protein Isolate and Klean Isolate are both NSF Certified for Sport. Naked Whey is the budget single-ingredient option."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can protein powder harm my baby?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A plain, well-tested protein powder used in normal amounts is not known to harm a developing baby. Potential harm comes from what else may be in a product: lead and other heavy metals, caffeine, unstudied herbs and very high vitamin A. Choosing an independently tested product with a short ingredient list addresses nearly all of those, and your OB can review the label with you."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey protein safe during pregnancy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, commercial whey is made from pasteurized milk and is an ordinary dairy protein, so it is generally considered safe in pregnancy unless you have a cow's milk allergy. Whey isolates are low in lactose and tend to test lower for heavy metals than plant proteins. Choose a certified product such as Thorne or Klean Isolate and avoid whey blends with stimulants or herbs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I avoid protein powders with artificial sweeteners while pregnant?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You do not have to. The FDA considers approved sweeteners such as sucralose, acesulfame potassium and stevia extracts safe at normal intakes, including in pregnancy. Some women prefer to limit them anyway, or find their taste changes. Unflavored options like Klean Isolate and Naked Whey contain no sweeteners, and Ritual and Truvani use non-artificial sweeteners, per the brands."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I have protein shakes in the first trimester?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if your provider agrees, and they can be especially useful when nausea and food aversions make meals hard. Your protein needs in the first trimester are close to your pre-pregnancy needs, so you rarely need much. Try small, cold servings, or an unflavored powder blended into fruit. If you cannot keep fluids down, contact your provider rather than relying on shakes."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder",
    tags: [
      "best protein powder for pregnancy",
      "protein powder for pregnancy",
      "is protein powder safe during pregnancy",
      "prenatal protein powder",
      "pregnancy protein shake"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Powder for Pregnancy</Fragment>
      Eight protein powders and shakes chosen for the things that matter when you are pregnant: independent contaminant testing, short labels with no herbs or stimulants, and a sensible protein dose. Talk to your OB or midwife before adding any supplement.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#at-a-glance">Comparison at a Glance</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#how-much-protein">Protein by Trimester</a>
      <a href="#is-it-safe">Is Protein Powder Safe?</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Heavy Metals</a>
      <a href="#avoid">Ingredients to Avoid</a>
      <a href="#whey-or-plant">Whey or Plant?</a>
