        <div id="how-much-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Protein Do You Need During Pregnancy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The official US figure comes from the Institute of Medicine, now the National Academy of Medicine, in its Dietary Reference Intakes. For adult women who are not pregnant, the Recommended Dietary Allowance is 0.8 g of protein per kilogram of body weight, which works out to about 46 g a day for a reference woman. For pregnancy, the IOM sets the RDA at 1.1 g per kilogram, or about 71 g a day, an increase of roughly 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            That extra 25 g is not spread evenly. The IOM based it on the second half of pregnancy, when the baby, placenta, uterus and blood volume grow fastest, and it did not set a higher requirement for the first trimester. In practice that means your protein needs in the first trimester are close to what they were before pregnancy, and the increase really starts to matter from the second trimester onward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protein needs by trimester</h3>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">IOM protein guidance</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Extra calories (ACOG)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Not pregnant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.8 g/kg, about 46 g/day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Baseline</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">First trimester</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No increase set by the IOM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No extra calories, for most women</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Second trimester</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.1 g/kg, about 71 g/day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 340 extra per day</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Third trimester</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.1 g/kg, about 71 g/day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 450 extra per day</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Some researchers think the official number is too low. A small 2015 study in the Journal of Nutrition, using a method called indicator amino acid oxidation, estimated needs of about 1.2 g per kilogram in early pregnancy and about 1.5 g per kilogram in late pregnancy. That is one study in a few dozen women, not a new guideline, but it is a reasonable argument for not undershooting. For a 70 kg woman, it would mean roughly 85 g a day in early pregnancy and just over 100 g a day near the end.
          </p>

          <p>
            The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists frames the calorie side simply: most women need no extra calories in the first trimester, about 340 extra a day in the second and about 450 in the third, with adjustments for twins and starting weight. One protein shake of 100-170 calories fits easily inside those numbers, which is why a scoop is a reasonable way to fill a protein gap without overshooting your energy needs. If you want a personal estimate, our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> gives a starting point to bring to your appointment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many women already eat close to 70 g of protein a day without trying, especially if they eat dairy, eggs, meat or legumes. Before you buy anything, it is worth logging a few typical days. If you are already at 75-90 g, a powder adds cost and little else. If nausea, aversions or a plant-based diet leave you at 50-60 g, a daily scoop closes most of the gap. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> explains the per-kilogram math in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-it-safe" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Protein Powder Safe During Pregnancy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein itself is not the concern. Whey, casein, pea and soy are ordinary food proteins that pregnant women eat every day in milk, yogurt, beans and tofu. The questions in pregnancy are about everything else that can end up in a tub: contaminants such as lead and cadmium, added herbs and stimulants, very high doses of certain vitamins, and how much quality control stands behind the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the US, protein powders are sold as dietary supplements or foods, and neither category requires the FDA to approve the product before it reaches shelves. Manufacturers are responsible for safety and for label accuracy, and enforcement mostly happens after problems appear. That is why third-party certification carries so much weight here: it is the only routine, independent check on what is actually inside. Our explainer on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers this regulatory gap in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            ACOG's general advice on supplements in pregnancy is to tell your ob-gyn about everything you take, including herbal products, because many have not been studied in pregnant women. A plain whey or pea powder with three or four ingredients is a very different product from a fat-burner protein with green tea extract and a proprietary herbal blend, and your care team will want to know which one you are using.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A plain, third-party tested protein powder is generally considered fine in pregnancy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The risk lives in the extras: herbs, stimulants, high-dose vitamin A, and untested products with unknown contaminant levels. Choose a short label with independent testing, keep it to one or two servings a day, and clear it with your OB or midwife.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Third-Party Testing and Heavy Metals Matter Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are the strongest reason to be choosy. Lead, cadmium and arsenic occur naturally in soil and water, and plants absorb them, so they can show up in protein powders, especially plant-based ones. The CDC states that no safe blood lead level has been identified in children, and lead can cross the placenta, which is why lowering avoidable exposure is a sensible goal during pregnancy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Independent testing has repeatedly found the same pattern. Consumer Reports' protein powder testing, most recently published in 2025, found that plant-based powders generally carried more lead than dairy-based ones, and some products in both groups exceeded the organization's level of concern per serving. Results vary by brand and even by batch, so no single test result is permanent, but the direction has been consistent. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide goes through the testing programs and what their limits mean.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the certification seals actually mean</h3>

          <p>
            NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Choice or Informed Sport were designed for athletes. They test for banned substances, and NSF Certified for Sport also checks label claims and screens for contaminants, including heavy metals. NSF Contents Certified checks that the label is accurate and that contaminants are within limits without the banned-substance testing. The Clean Label Project runs its own contaminant testing. None of these programs certifies a product as safe for pregnancy specifically, but each one means someone other than the manufacturer has looked inside the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            When a product has no seal, you can still ask. Reputable brands will send a certificate of analysis, a lab report for a specific batch, if you email them with the lot number from your tub. Look for lead, cadmium, arsenic and mercury results and the lab's name. A brand that cannot or will not share recent results has told you something useful. Products carrying a California Proposition 65 warning are not necessarily dangerous, but the warning does mean the company could not show the product was below that state's strict thresholds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ingredients to Avoid in a Pregnancy Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of what you should avoid has nothing to do with the protein. Read the whole label, including the small print under any proprietary blend, and skip products with the following.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Caffeine and stimulants</h3>

          <p>
            ACOG advises keeping caffeine below 200 mg a day during pregnancy. Coffee-flavored proteins, energy proteins and anything marketed for fat burning often contain caffeine, green tea extract, guarana, yerba mate or other stimulants, sometimes without stating the dose. Those amounts add to your coffee and tea and can push you past the limit without noticing. Choose a product with no stimulant ingredients at all and keep your caffeine where you can count it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Herbal blends and adaptogens</h3>

          <p>
            Ashwagandha, maca, black cohosh, dong quai, licorice root, high-dose turmeric extracts, so-called hormone-balancing blends and detox herbs are common in wellness powders. Most have not been studied for safety in pregnancy, and some, such as ashwagandha, are flagged by US health agencies as possibly unsafe during pregnancy. The simplest approach is to choose a powder with no herbs at all, which every pick on this list satisfies.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">High-dose vitamin A and organ powders</h3>

          <p>
            Preformed vitamin A, listed as retinol or retinyl palmitate, can cause birth defects at high doses, and the tolerable upper intake in pregnancy is 3,000 mcg a day. Meal-replacement shakes with full vitamin premixes, stacked on top of a prenatal vitamin, can add up. So-called ancestral protein powders that include desiccated liver are worth particular caution, because liver is an extremely concentrated source of vitamin A. Beta-carotene from plants does not carry the same risk.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Raw or unpasteurized ingredients</h3>

          <p>
            Pregnant women are advised to avoid unpasteurized milk and cheese because of the risk of Listeria. Commercial whey and casein powders are made from pasteurized milk, and bottled shakes such as Core Power are pasteurized too. Be wary of anything marketed as raw dairy protein and confirm the processing with the brand. On plant labels, raw usually means low-heat or sprouted rather than unpasteurized, but the same rule applies: if you are unsure, ask or skip it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whey-or-plant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Whey or Plant Protein When You Are Pregnant?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both work. Whey is a complete protein that is rich in leucine and digests easily, and isolates contain very little lactose. It also tends to come out lower in heavy metal testing, and more whey products carry strong certifications. If dairy agrees with you, a certified whey isolate is the simplest, best-documented choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins make sense if you have a cow's milk allergy, follow a vegan diet, or simply find dairy unappealing right now. Pea protein is the most common base, and blends with rice, pumpkin seed or chia round out the amino acid profile. Ritual's pregnancy shake and Truvani are both pea-led. The trade-off is that the average plant protein carries more lead and cadmium than the average whey, so independent testing matters more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soy protein is safe to eat in pregnancy in normal food amounts, and soy foods have a long history in diets around the world. Soy isolate powders are less common now, and some people prefer to keep soy to whole foods such as tofu and edamame during pregnancy; that is a preference rather than a rule. Collagen is not unsafe, but it is not a complete protein, so it is a poor primary protein source for pregnancy. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> comparison breaks down the differences in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Use Protein Powder Safely in Pregnancy
          </h2>

          <p>
            One serving a day is enough for most people who need a powder at all. A scoop of 20-25 g closes a typical gap without crowding out meals, and it keeps your exposure to any trace contaminants low. Two servings can make sense for a short spell of severe aversions, but at that point it is worth involving a registered dietitian.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep taking your prenatal vitamin. No protein powder on this list replaces folic acid, iron, iodine or DHA, and Ritual's choline is a helpful extra rather than a substitute. Check that any vitamin-fortified shake does not double up on nutrients with your prenatal, especially vitamin A.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, bring the tub or a photo of its label to your next appointment. It takes your OB or midwife a few seconds to look over an ingredient list, and that conversation is the most reliable safety check you have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="special-cases" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Nausea, Gestational Diabetes and Other Special Cases
          </h2>

          <p>
            Morning sickness is one of the most common reasons women reach for protein powder. Cold drinks are often easier than hot food, small sips through the day go down better than a full glass, and unflavored powders hidden in a fruit smoothie or popsicle avoid the cloying taste of a sweet shake. A ready-to-drink bottle kept in the fridge removes the effort entirely. If you cannot keep fluids down or are losing weight, call your provider, because severe vomiting needs medical care rather than supplements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gestational diabetes changes the priorities. Choose a powder with little or no sugar, pair it with fiber and fat, and coordinate with your care team or diabetes educator, who may want shakes timed around meals and glucose checks. Ritual, Klean Isolate and Gold Standard all come in at about 1 g of sugar or less per serving per their labels. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-diabetics/">protein powder for diabetics</a> guide explains how to read a label for blood sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kidney disease, a history of preeclampsia, food allergies and any prescribed diet are all reasons to get individual advice before adding a supplement. The same applies if you are carrying twins or more, because protein and calorie needs rise further and your team may set specific targets.
          </p>

          <p>
            After the birth, needs change again, especially if you breastfeed. Our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-breastfeeding/">best protein powder for breastfeeding</a> covers lactation protein and calorie needs and the evidence on lactation add-ins such as fenugreek.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a fat-burner or energy protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Products marketed for weight loss or energy often hide caffeine, green tea extract or herbal blends. In pregnancy, those ingredients add caffeine you are not counting and herbs that have not been studied. Choose a plain protein with no stimulant or herbal ingredients at all, and keep coffee and tea as your only, measurable caffeine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring third-party testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pretty label tells you nothing about lead or cadmium. Without NSF, Informed or similar independent testing, you are relying on the manufacturer alone. If a product you like has no seal, email the brand for a recent certificate of analysis for your batch, and move on if they will not share one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a shake instead of a prenatal vitamin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein powders do not supply the folic acid, iron, iodine and DHA a prenatal provides, even when they add a few vitamins. Keep taking your prenatal, and check that any fortified shake does not double up on vitamin A or other nutrients with upper limits you could exceed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling up on vitamin A</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Meal-replacement shakes and liver-based ancestral powders can contain large amounts of preformed vitamin A. Stacked on a prenatal, that can approach the 3,000 mcg daily upper limit for pregnancy. Read the vitamin panel, look for retinol or retinyl palmitate, and ask your provider if you are unsure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking several shakes a day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                More protein is not automatically better, and every extra serving adds cost and trace contaminant exposure while crowding out meals with fiber, iron and omega-3s. One scoop a day fills a typical gap. If you think you need more, ask a registered dietitian to look at your whole diet first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

