          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is it safe to drink protein shakes while pregnant?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most women, a plain, third-party tested protein powder is considered fine in pregnancy, but there is no blanket approval and you should check with your OB or midwife. The main risks come from contaminants such as lead, from herbal or stimulant add-ins, and from high doses of vitamin A, not from the protein itself. Pick a short label with independent testing and keep it to one serving a day.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein do I need when pregnant?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The Institute of Medicine sets the pregnancy RDA at 1.1 g per kilogram of body weight, about 71 g a day for a reference woman, compared with about 46 g before pregnancy. The increase applies mainly to the second and third trimesters. Some newer research suggests needs could be higher late in pregnancy, around 1.5 g per kilogram, so aiming a little above the RDA is reasonable.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for pregnancy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Our top pick is Ritual Essential Protein Daily Shake Pregnancy and Postpartum, because it was designed for pregnancy, gives 20 g of pea protein and 250 mg of choline per serving per its label, and contains no herbs or stimulants. If you prefer dairy, Thorne Whey Protein Isolate and Klean Isolate are both NSF Certified for Sport. Naked Whey is the budget single-ingredient option.
                </p>
